<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rozafa Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rozafa Castle is a fortress on a rocky hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> in northern Albania, near the meeting of the Buna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> rivers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Shkodër</a>. Originally an Illyrian stronghold, it was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the siege of 1478–79. It takes its name from a legend of a young woman walled into its foundations so that the walls would stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rozafa-castle.jpg" alt="Rozafa Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bdx · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rozafa_Castle_in_July_2013_(5).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
